\chapter{Numeric, text, sequence, and functional APIs}
\label{ch:52}
\index{Numeric, text, sequence, and functional APIs}

The source wrappers add conventions on top of the core types. \OnxCodeTerm{Int} has division, remainder, and modulus behavior that needs separate tests for negative operands and an overflow boundary. \OnxCodeTerm{String.find} reports a byte position or negative one, so the next step after a found index must still respect UTF-8 boundaries when making a character-level decision. Ascending tabulation and left or right folds give different observable callback orders.

\section{Compose a small data transformation}
Take a string, test a prefix, and return an optional byte position. Use \OnxCodeTerm{Functional.compose(f,g)} only when the intermediate result matches the parameter type of \OnxCodeTerm{f}; the call applies \OnxCodeTerm{g} first. For a list fold, use an order-sensitive test such as building a string of visited items. Test an empty list and an incorrectly shaped S-expression in addition to the ordinary path. Byte-level search, list traversal and persistent S-expression allocation have different cost models.

\section{Pair each quotient with its own remainder}
Take seven units of debt and divide them into groups of three. For arguments minus seven and three, truncation toward zero produces a quotient of minus two. Multiplying it back by three gives minus six, so the remainder needed to recover the dividend is minus one. These are the answers from \OnxCodeTerm{quot} and \OnxCodeTerm{rem}. Floor division chooses the next smaller integer, minus three. Its product is minus nine; the residual is therefore positive two. These are the components returned by \OnxCodeTerm{divmod}. A caller pairing the floor quotient with the truncating remainder would reconstruct minus ten rather than the input.

Now reverse both signs. Seven divided by minus three still has floor quotient minus three, but multiplying back gives positive nine. This time the modulus is minus two: for a nonzero modulus the divisor determines its sign. With an exact multiple there is no adjustment. The source computes a truncating quotient first and adjusts it only for opposite signs and a nonzero remainder. It also treats a divisor of minus one specially in the remainder helper; obtaining a zero remainder need not require forming an unrepresentable positive quotient for the minimum integer.

As an application, grouping positions into consecutive bins of width three should put minus one in bin minus one and zero in bin zero. Use floor division and keep the modulus as a nonnegative within-bin offset. Checking just the positive positions would not reveal a mistaken choice of division family.

The within-bin offset for position minus one is two. Moving one position to the right resets that offset to zero and advances the bin, as it does at every other boundary.

